 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 5.03 — Invariant Metrics and Homogeneous Spaces\n\n5.01 and 5.02 treated $G$ as an algebraic object. This notebook puts a **metric** on it and hands the whole of Stage 4 back.\n\n**Left-invariant metrics.** Choose any inner product $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle$ on $\\mathfrak g = T_eG$ and push it around by left translation: $\\langle u,v\\rangle_g := \\langle (dL_{g^{-1}})u, (dL_{g^{-1}})v\\rangle_e$. Every choice gives a Riemannian metric, and $G$ acts on itself by isometries. So a Lie group has as many left-invariant metrics as $\\mathfrak g$ has inner products — a whole cone of them, most with nothing special about them.\n\n**Bi-invariant metrics** are the good ones: invariant under left *and* right translation, equivalently under $\\mathrm{Ad}$:\n\n$$\\langle \\mathrm{Ad}_g X, \\mathrm{Ad}_g Y\\rangle = \\langle X, Y\\rangle \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\langle [Z,X], Y\\rangle + \\langle X, [Z,Y]\\rangle = 0.$$\n\nThe infinitesimal form on the right says $\\mathrm{ad}_Z$ is **skew-adjoint**. Such a metric exists precisely when $G$ is compact (up to abelian factors), and then everything simplifies at once:\n\n$$\\nabla_XY = \\tfrac12[X,Y], \\qquad R(X,Y)Z = \\tfrac14[[X,Y],Z], \\qquad K(X,Y) = \\tfrac{\\tfrac14\\big|[X,Y]\\big|^2}{|X|^2|Y|^2 - \\langle X,Y\\rangle^2}.$$\n\n**Curvature is the bracket.** $K \\ge 0$ always, and $K = 0$ exactly where $X$ and $Y$ commute. All of Stage 4's machinery collapses into algebra — no Christoffel symbols to compute, no coordinates to choose. And the group exponential of 5.02 coincides with the Riemannian $\\exp_e$ of 4.03: **one-parameter subgroups are geodesics**.\n\n**The Killing form.**\n\n$$B(X,Y) = \\operatorname{tr}(\\mathrm{ad}_X\\,\\mathrm{ad}_Y)$$\n\nis the canonical $\\mathrm{Ad}$-invariant bilinear form, built from nothing but the bracket. Cartan's criterion: $\\mathfrak g$ is semisimple iff $B$ is non-degenerate, and **compact** iff $B$ is negative definite. So $-B$ is a bi-invariant metric on a compact semisimple group, and 5.01's observation — compactness tracking the sign of curvature — becomes a computation.\n\n**Homogeneous spaces.** If $G$ acts transitively on $M$ with stabiliser $H$ at a point, then $M \\cong G/H$. This is Klein's Erlangen program (2.01) in Riemannian dress: the space is *reconstructed* from the group. Spheres, projective spaces and Grassmannians all arise this way, and a $G$-invariant metric on $G/H$ corresponds to an $\\mathrm{Ad}(H)$-invariant inner product on $\\mathfrak g/\\mathfrak h$.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\nlinsolvewarn : false$    /* else every coords() call reports eliminated\n                            dependent equations — hundreds of lines */\nbr(X, Y) := ratsimp( X.Y - Y.X )$\n\n/* coordinates of a matrix in a given basis of the algebra */\ncoords(M, basis) := block([n_, cs, eqs, sol],\n  n_ : length(basis),\n  cs : makelist( concat(kk_, i), i, 1, n_),\n  eqs : flatten( args( ratsimp( sum(cs[i]*basis[i], i, 1, n_) - M ) ) ),\n  sol : linsolve(eqs, cs),\n  makelist( rhs(sol[i]), i, 1, n_) )$\n\n/* matrix of ad_X in that basis: column j is the image of basis[j] */\nadmat(X, basis) := transpose( apply(matrix,\n  makelist( coords( br(X, basis[j]), basis), j, 1, length(basis)) ) )$\n\n/* the Killing form B(X,Y) = tr(ad_X ad_Y) */\nkilling(basis) := block([n_], n_ : length(basis),\n  genmatrix( lambda([i,j], ratsimp( mat_trace(\n      admat(basis[i], basis) . admat(basis[j], basis) ) )), n_, n_) )$\n\nreport(basis, label) := block([B],\n  B : killing(basis),\n  print(\"   \", label, \" :  B = \", B),\n  print(\"      det B = \", ratsimp(determinant(B)),\n        \"     eigenvalues = \", eigenvalues(B)[1]) )$\n\nprint(\"Killing forms B(X,Y) = tr(ad_X ad_Y):\")$\nprint(\"\")$\n\n/* so(3) */\nLb : [ matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,-1],[0,1,0]),\n       matrix([0,0,1],[0,0,0],[-1,0,0]),\n       matrix([0,-1,0],[1,0,0],[0,0,0]) ]$\nreport(Lb, \"so(3)  \")$\n\n/* su(2) */\nEb : [ matrix([0,-%i/2],[-%i/2,0]),\n       matrix([0,-1/2],[1/2,0]),\n       matrix([-%i/2,0],[0,%i/2]) ]$\nreport(Eb, \"su(2)  \")$\n\n/* sl(2,ℝ), basis h, e, f */\nSb : [ matrix([1,0],[0,-1]), matrix([0,1],[0,0]), matrix([0,0],[1,0]) ]$\nreport(Sb, \"sl(2,ℝ)\")$\n\n/* the affine \"ax+b\" algebra: 2-dimensional, spanned by the translation\n   generator X = E₁₂ and the dilation generator Y = E₁₁ */\nAb : [ matrix([0,1],[0,0]), matrix([1,0],[0,0]) ]$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   the affine algebra of x ↦ ax+b:  [X,Y] = \",\n      coords(br(Ab[1],Ab[2]), Ab), \" in the basis (X,Y), i.e. [Y,X] = X —\")$\nprint(\"   the 2-dimensional non-abelian algebra, unique up to isomorphism.\")$\nreport(Ab, \"aff(1) \")$\n\n/* Heisenberg: strictly upper triangular 3×3, nilpotent */\nHb : [ matrix([0,1,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0]),\n       matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,1],[0,0,0]),\n       matrix([0,0,1],[0,0,0],[0,0,0]) ]$\nreport(Hb, \"heis   \")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"CARTAN'S CRITERIA, read off the table:\")$\nprint(\"   so(3), su(2) : B = −2·I, negative definite ⟹ semisimple AND\")$\nprint(\"                  compact.  −B is a bi-invariant metric.\")$\nprint(\"   sl(2,ℝ)      : non-degenerate (det = −128) but INDEFINITE,\")$\nprint(\"                  eigenvalues −4, 4, 8 ⟹ semisimple, not compact.\")$\nprint(\"                  No bi-invariant Riemannian metric exists.\")$\nprint(\"   aff(1), heis : B degenerate ⟹ not semisimple.  For Heisenberg,\")$\nprint(\"                  B vanishes identically — nilpotent algebras are\")$\nprint(\"                  invisible to the Killing form.\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"5.01 observed that compactness tracked the sign of the curvature.\")$\nprint(\"The Killing form is what makes that a computation rather than a\")$\nprint(\"coincidence.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 2,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Killing forms B(X,Y) = tr(ad_X ad_Y):\n    so(3)    :  B =  matrix([-2,0,0],[0,-2,0],[0,0,-2])\n      det B =  -8      eigenvalues =  [-2]\n    su(2)    :  B =  matrix([-2,0,0],[0,-2,0],[0,0,-2])\n      det B =  -8      eigenvalues =  [-2]\n    sl(2,ℝ)  :  B =  matrix([8,0,0],[0,0,4],[0,4,0])\n      det B =  -128      eigenvalues =  [-4,4,8]\n   the affine algebra of x ↦ ax+b:  [X,Y] =\n  [-1,0]  in the basis (X,Y), i.e. [Y,X] = X —\n   the 2-dimensional non-abelian algebra, unique up to isomorphism.\n    aff(1)   :  B =  matrix([0,0],[0,1])\n      det B =  0      eigenvalues =  [0,1]\n    heis     :  B =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])\n      det B =  0      eigenvalues =  [0]\nCARTAN'S CRITERIA, read off the table:\n   so(3), su(2) : B = −2·I, negative definite ⟹ semisimple AND\n                  compact.  −B is a bi-invariant metric.\n   sl(2,ℝ)      : non-degenerate (det = −128) but INDEFINITE,\n                  eigenvalues −4, 4, 8 ⟹ semisimple, not compact.\n                  No bi-invariant Riemannian metric exists.\n   aff(1), heis : B degenerate ⟹ not semisimple.  For Heisenberg,\n                  B vanishes identically — nilpotent algebras are\n                  invisible to the Killing form.\n5.01 observed that compactness tracked the sign of the curvature.\nThe Killing form is what makes that a computation rather than a\ncoincidence."
     ]
    }
