     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "aff(1): ad_X =  matrix([0,-1],[0,0])    ad_Y =  matrix([1,0],[0,0])\n   bi-invariance equations:  [0,-pp,-pp,-(2*qq),2*pp,qq,qq,0]\n   solution:  [pp = 0,qq = 0,rr = %r1]\n   p = q = 0 forced, so G = diag(0, r): DEGENERATE.\n   No bi-invariant inner product on aff(1) is positive definite,\n   matching the degenerate Killing form found above.\nleft translation L_{(a₀,b₀)}(a,b) = (a₀a, a₀b + b₀), Jacobian\n  matrix([a0,0],[0,a0])\n   pullback of g − g =  matrix([0,0],[0,0])\n   so ds² = (da² + db²)/a² is LEFT-invariant.\n   And that is the hyperbolic metric of 2.06 and 4.07, with (b,a)\n   the upper half-plane coordinates.\n   K =  -1    — the hyperbolic plane, K = −1 ✓\nA bi-invariant metric forces K = ¼|[X,Y]|²/(...) ≥ 0.  Here K = −1,\nso this left-invariant metric CANNOT be bi-invariant — which the\nlinear system above already showed directly.  Three independent\nroutes to the same fact:\n   • the Killing form of aff(1) is degenerate\n   • the bi-invariance equations force a degenerate G\n   • the curvature is negative, and bi-invariant ⟹ K ≥ 0\nSo the hyperbolic plane is a LIE GROUP with a left-invariant metric,\nas well as the homogeneous space PSL₂(ℝ)/SO(2).  Two descriptions of\nthe same surface, and 2.06 used neither."
     ]
    }
