  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- Homogeneous spaces M = G/H --- */\ndSO(n)  := n*(n-1)/2$\ndU(n)   := n^2$\ndSU(n)  := n^2 - 1$\ndSUkn(k, n) := k^2 + (n-k)^2 - 1$        /* S(U(k) × U(n−k)) */\n\nprint(\"dim M = dim G − dim H, checked against the known dimension:\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   Sⁿ = SO(n+1)/SO(n)\")$\nprint(\"      n    dim G   dim H   difference   n\")$\nfor n : 2 thru 6 do\n  print(\"     \", n, \"     \", dSO(n+1), \"      \", dSO(n), \"       \",\n        dSO(n+1)-dSO(n), \"         \", n)$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   Gr(k,n) = O(n)/(O(k)×O(n−k)),  real Grassmannian\")$\nprint(\"      k  n    dim G   dim H   difference   k(n−k)\")$\nfor n : 2 thru 5 do for k : 1 thru n-1 do\n  print(\"     \", k, \" \", n, \"     \", dSO(n), \"      \",\n        dSO(k)+dSO(n-k), \"       \", dSO(n)-dSO(k)-dSO(n-k),\n        \"         \", k*(n-k))$\nprint(\"      (k = 1 gives ℝPⁿ⁻¹, of dimension n−1, as it should.)\")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   ℂPⁿ = SU(n+1)/S(U(n)×U(1)),  real dimension 2n\")$\nprint(\"      n    dim G   dim H   difference   2n\")$\nfor n : 1 thru 5 do\n  print(\"     \", n, \"     \", dSU(n+1), \"      \", dSUkn(n, n+1), \"       \",\n        dSU(n+1)-dSUkn(n,n+1), \"         \", 2*n)$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"THE ERLANGEN TRIO, all with the SAME stabiliser SO(2):\")$\nprint(\"      space   G            dim G   H       dim H   dim M\")$\nprint(\"      E²      E(2)             3    SO(2)      1      \", 3-1)$\nprint(\"      S²      SO(3)            3    SO(2)      1      \", 3-1)$\nprint(\"      H²      PSL₂(ℝ)          3    SO(2)      1      \", 3-1)$\nprint(\"   Three geometries, three groups of the same dimension, the same\")$\nprint(\"   isotropy group.  What distinguishes them is not the dimension\")$\nprint(\"   count but the ALGEBRA — compact, flat or non-compact — which is\")$\nprint(\"   exactly what the Killing form measured above.\")$\n\n/* --- transitivity, constructively --- */\n/* SO(3) acts transitively on S²: build a rotation taking e₃ to any unit v. */\nhat3(v) := matrix([0,-v[3],v[2]],[v[3],0,-v[1]],[-v[2],v[1],0])$\nrot_to(v) := block([e3, ax, s, cc, K],\n  /* Rodrigues about the axis e₃ × v through the angle between them */\n  e3 : [0,0,1],\n  ax : [ e3[2]*v[3]-e3[3]*v[2], e3[3]*v[1]-e3[1]*v[3], e3[1]*v[2]-e3[2]*v[1] ],\n  s  : sqrt( ax[1]^2 + ax[2]^2 + ax[3]^2 ),\n  cc : v[3],                       /* e₃ · v */\n  K  : hat3(ax),\n  ratsimp( ident(3) + K + K^^2 * (1-cc)/s^2 ) )$\n\nvtest : [3/13, 4/13, 12/13]$        /* a unit vector: 9 + 16 + 144 = 169 */\nRt : rot_to(vtest)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"transitivity on S², target v = \", vtest, \" :\")$\nprint(\"   R = \", Rt)$\nprint(\"   R e₃ − v = \", ratsimp( Rt . transpose(matrix([0,0,1]))\n                                 - transpose(matrix(vtest)) ))$\nprint(\"   RᵀR − I = \", ratsimp( transpose(Rt).Rt - ident(3) ),\n      \"   det R = \", ratsimp(determinant(Rt)))$\nprint(\"   So SO(3) reaches every point of S², and the stabiliser of e₃ is\")$\nprint(\"   the rotations about the z-axis: SO(2).  Hence S² = SO(3)/SO(2),\")$\nprint(\"   with no coordinates and no metric used in the argument.\")$\nprint(\"   That is Klein's construction (2.01) — the space rebuilt from the\")$\nprint(\"   group — now carrying a Riemannian metric as well.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 5,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "dim M = dim G − dim H, checked against the known dimension:\n   Sⁿ = SO(n+1)/SO(n)\n      n    dim G   dim H   difference   n\n      2       3        1         2           2\n      3       6        3         3           3\n      4       10        6         4           4\n      5       15        10         5           5\n      6       21        15         6           6\n   Gr(k,n) = O(n)/(O(k)×O(n−k)),  real Grassmannian\n      k  n    dim G   dim H   difference   k(n−k)\n      1   2       1        0         1           1\n      1   3       3        1         2           2\n      2   3       3        1         2           2\n      1   4       6        3         3           3\n      2   4       6        2         4           4\n      3   4       6        3         3           3\n      1   5       10        6         4           4\n      2   5       10        4         6           6\n      3   5       10        4         6           6\n      4   5       10        6         4           4\n      (k = 1 gives ℝPⁿ⁻¹, of dimension n−1, as it should.)\n   ℂPⁿ = SU(n+1)/S(U(n)×U(1)),  real dimension 2n\n      n    dim G   dim H   difference   2n\n      1       3        1         2           2\n      2       8        4         4           4\n      3       15        9         6           6\n      4       24        16         8           8\n      5       35        25         10           10\nTHE ERLANGEN TRIO, all with the SAME stabiliser SO(2):\n      space   G            dim G   H       dim H   dim M\n      E²      E(2)             3    SO(2)      1       2\n      S²      SO(3)            3    SO(2)      1       2\n      H²      PSL₂(ℝ)          3    SO(2)      1       2\n   Three geometries, three groups of the same dimension, the same\n   isotropy group.  What distinguishes them is not the dimension\n   count but the ALGEBRA — compact, flat or non-compact — which is\n   exactly what the Killing form measured above.\ntransitivity on S², target v =  [3/13,4/13,12/13]  :\n   R =\n       matrix([316/325,-(12/325),3/13],[-(12/325),309/325,4/13],\n              [-(3/13),-(4/13),12/13])\n   R e₃ − v =  matrix([0],[0],[0])\n   RᵀR − I =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])    det R =  1\n   So SO(3) reaches every point of S², and the stabiliser of e₃ is\n   the rotations about the z-axis: SO(2).  Hence S² = SO(3)/SO(2),\n   with no coordinates and no metric used in the argument.\n   That is Klein's construction (2.01) — the space rebuilt from the\n   group — now carrying a Riemannian metric as well."
