   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\n$$B(X,Y) = \\operatorname{tr}(\\mathrm{ad}_X\\mathrm{ad}_Y), \\qquad K(X,Y) = \\frac{\\tfrac14\\big|[X,Y]\\big|^2}{|X|^2|Y|^2 - \\langle X,Y\\rangle^2}$$\n\n**Cartan's criteria, as a table.** The Killing form was computed from structure constants alone for five algebras:\n\n| algebra | $B$ | verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$, $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ | $-2I$, eigenvalues all $-2$ | semisimple **and compact** |\n| $\\mathfrak{sl}(2,\\mathbb R)$ | $\\det = -128$, eigenvalues $-4,4,8$ | semisimple, **not** compact |\n| $\\mathfrak{aff}(1)$ | $\\operatorname{diag}(0,1)$, degenerate | not semisimple |\n| Heisenberg | identically $0$ | not semisimple — nilpotent algebras are invisible to $B$ |\n\n5.01 noticed compactness tracking the sign of curvature. This is the mechanism.\n\n**Curvature is literally the bracket.** With $\\langle X,Y\\rangle = -2\\operatorname{tr}(XY)$ on $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ — verified orthonormal, and verified bi-invariant with **zero violations out of 27 triples** — the sectional curvature came out $\\tfrac14$ on every basis pair *and for a fully generic pair of elements*. Constant curvature, computed with no metric in coordinates, no Christoffel symbols, no Riemann tensor.\n\nThe reason the answer is exactly $\\tfrac14$ and not a function of position: under 5.01's hat map $[X,Y]$ corresponds to $x \\times y$, and $|x \\times y|^2 = |x|^2|y|^2 - (x\\cdot y)^2$ is precisely the denominator. Numerator and denominator cancel.\n\n$K = \\tfrac14$ means radius 2. Rescaling the metric by $\\tfrac14$ gives $K = 1$ and scalar curvature $S = n(n-1)K = 6$ — the value 4.05 obtained for the unit 3-sphere from `ctensor` and a hand-rolled Riemann tensor, by a completely different route.\n\nAnd since $\\nabla_XX = \\tfrac12[X,X] = 0$, **one-parameter subgroups are geodesics**: 5.02's group exponential and 4.03's Riemannian $\\exp_e$ are the same map.\n\n**The hyperbolic plane is a Lie group.** The $ax+b$ group carries $ds^2 = (da^2+db^2)/a^2$, verified left-invariant by pulling back along $L_{(a_0,b_0)}$ (difference zero), and Stage 4's machinery gives $K = -1$. It is 2.06's upper half-plane, arrived at from group theory rather than from Möbius maps or from a metric written down by hand.\n\nIt has **no** bi-invariant metric, and this notebook shows that three independent ways:\n\n- the Killing form of $\\mathfrak{aff}(1)$ is degenerate;\n- the bi-invariance equations $\\mathrm{ad}_Z^TG + G\\,\\mathrm{ad}_Z = 0$ force $p = q = 0$, so $G = \\operatorname{diag}(0,r)$ — solved explicitly here;\n- bi-invariance would force $K \\ge 0$, and $K = -1$.\n\n**Homogeneous spaces.** $\\dim G - \\dim H$ matched the known dimension in every row: $S^n = SO(n+1)/SO(n)$, the real Grassmannians $O(n)/(O(k)\\times O(n-k))$ with $k(n-k)$, and $\\mathbb{CP}^n = SU(n+1)/S(U(n)\\times U(1))$ with $2n$.\n\nTransitivity was checked constructively rather than asserted: for $v = (3,4,12)/13$ a Rodrigues rotation was built with $Re_3 = v$ exactly, $R^TR = I$, $\\det R = 1$. The stabiliser of $e_3$ is the rotations about the $z$-axis, so $S^2 = SO(3)/SO(2)$ — no coordinates, no metric.\n\n**The Erlangen trio, one last time.** $\\mathbb{E}^2 = E(2)/SO(2)$, $S^2 = SO(3)/SO(2)$, $H^2 = PSL_2(\\mathbb R)/SO(2)$: three groups of dimension 3, the same isotropy group $SO(2)$, three different geometries. The dimension count cannot tell them apart; the Killing form can.\n\n## Next\n\n**5.04 — Symmetric Spaces** axiomatises what $S^n$, $H^n$ and the Grassmannians have in common: at every point there is an isometry reversing geodesics through it. Curvature then comes from the bracket alone, $R(X,Y)Z = -[[X,Y],Z]$, and compact and non-compact types pair off in a duality that sends $S^n$ to $H^n$.\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. [doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9). Chapter 3 for invariant metrics and homogeneous spaces; Chapter 7 Problem set for the bi-invariant curvature formula.\n- Milnor, J. (1976) 'Curvatures of left invariant metrics on Lie groups', *Advances in Mathematics*, 21(3), pp. 293–329. [doi:10.1016/S0001-8708(76)80002-3](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0001-8708(76)80002-3). The definitive treatment, and the source of most of what is known about left-invariant metrics that are not bi-invariant — including the $ax+b$ example.\n- Helgason, S. (1978) *Differential Geometry, Lie Groups, and Symmetric Spaces*. New York: Academic Press. Chapters 2 and 4. The standard reference for the Killing form, Cartan's criteria and $G/H$.\n- do Carmo, M.P. (1992) *Riemannian Geometry*. Boston: Birkhäuser. Chapter 3 exercises 1–4 develop bi-invariant metrics from scratch at exactly this level.\n- Besse, A.L. (1987) *Einstein Manifolds*. Berlin: Springer. Chapter 7 for homogeneous Einstein metrics, if the next question is which $G/H$ carry them.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell was executed. `linsolvewarn : false` is set in the first cell because the coordinate solver otherwise emits several hundred lines of \"dependent equations eliminated\".*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ]
